cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use red-first-review or mutmatrix in research or software, please cite it as below."
title: "red-first-review-skill (mutmatrix)"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Dziatkovskii
    given-names: Anton
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7408-3054"
    affiliation: "Palo Alto AI Research Lab"
repository-code: "https://github.com/tonydzi/red-first-review-skill"
url: "https://github.com/tonydzi/red-first-review-skill"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A stdlib-only Python tool and Claude Code skill for reviewing a pull request
  by measuring which of the guards it adds would survive being deleted. Each
  guard is neutralised in turn, the project's own test suite is run, and the
  mutations nothing notices are reported as the finding. Most of the code is
  not the mutation loop but the refusals that stop it printing an unsupportable
  table: a baseline check (a suite already red attributes nothing to the PR), a
  canary check (a test command that never imports the mutated file makes every
  kill another test's), a compile check (an unparseable mutant fails every test
  and looks exactly like perfect coverage), and an UNPROVEN verdict when the
  canary is skipped. Exit 0 no survivors, 1 survivors, 3 the run measured
  nothing and must not be quoted. The test suite ships a kill-list and a
  documented red mode, so the checker is itself shown failing before it is
  trusted.
